      {
        "id": 229896,
        "name": "mistergone",
        "username": "mistergone",
        "avatar_template": "/user_avatar/forums.greaterthangames.com/mistergone/{size}/1898_2.png",
        "created_at": "2020-03-04T14:32:59.000Z",
        "cooked": "<p>I would second catDreaming:</p>\n<p><strong>Punishment </strong>is probably only a bonus for that single enemy. Let's say you have a -1 penalty from Villain A, and you have a -2 penalty from Villain B, and there's also some minions. Then, you Attack multiple with the Mid die. You apply both penalties to the Mid die, so your Mid result is -3 against the minions,&nbsp;-1 against Villain A, and +1 Against Villain B. \"Attack multiple\" gets weird fast, but I think this is how I'd run it.</p>\n<p>For <strong>Frontline Fighting</strong>, it says \"the Attack action,\" and when any character performs an Action on their turn, that Action may be an Ability or Basic Action (by the steps outlined in Chapter 2). I assume enemies follow those exact steps, as well. So the Villain, if capable of the Attack action, must take it... but they can still use their Abilities, modifiers, etc. I don't see a distinction between \"<em>the</em>&nbsp;Attack Action\" and&nbsp;\"<em>an</em>&nbsp;Attack Action\" here, but I can totally see where you might infer it. I like strictly constructed language in gaming rules! I don't think these rules use very strictly constructed language, though, and I am okay with that. :)</p>",
        "post_number": 3,
        "post_type": 1,
        "updated_at": "2021-02-07T06:04:45.735Z",
